%method
@title
Experimental process
@paragraph

%method
@title
Experimental process
@paragraph

@subtitle
number = 1
10X Chromium single cell technology principle
@paragraph
10X Chromium technology is a droplet microfluidic system based on water-in-oil emulsion. Through the introduction of Gel Beads-in-Emulsion (GEM) technology, gel beads carrying oligonucleotide tags are wrapped together with a single cell or nucleus in water-in-oil droplets. Each GEM serves as an independent micro-reactor for cell lysis and capture of mRNA molecules, as well as the tag sequence used to identify beads from the same GEM. The oligonucleotides on the gel bead surface contain: Cell Barcode (16bp) for identifying cells, UMI (10bp for v2, 12bp for v3) for identifying molecules, and Poly(dT) sequence for capturing mRNA. Library construction and Illumina sequencing of cDNA can obtain gene expression information of a large number of cells at one time, as shown in the figure below.
@figure
number = 2
title = "Schematic diagram of 10X Chromium single-cell technique."
file = <url = resource/scRNA_library_structure.png>
size = <width = 700; height = 300>
desc = " "

@subtitle
number = 2
Sequencing library structure
@paragraph
The cDNA was amplified by PCR, fragmented, end-repaired with A-tailing, ligated to adapters, re-amplified, and constructed as a standard library for Illumina sequencing, then sequenced by computer. The library structure is shown in the figure below.
@figure
number = 3
title = "Schematic diagram of single-cell sequencing library structure."
file = <url = resource/scRNA_library_sequence.png>
size = <width = 700; height = 300>
desc = "Cell Barcode: Synthetic nucleic acid sequence used to identify cells (16bp); UMI: Used to identify nucleic acid sequences captured from sample tissue (10bp for v2, 12bp for v3); Poly(dT): Sequence for complementary pairing with mRNA poly(A); Insert: Captured RNA fragment"

%method
@title
Information analysis process
@paragraph
The raw data obtained by sequencing are called raw reads. The Cell Ranger analysis pipeline provided by 10X Genomics is used to process the offline data, mainly including the following steps: (1) Align Read2 to the reference genome using STAR; (2) Correct Cell Barcodes against the whitelist and correct UMIs from Read1; (3) Annotate aligned reads to corresponding genes; (4) Deduplicate and count by valid Barcode+UMI+Gene combinations; (5) Distinguish real cells from empty droplets using the EmptyDrops algorithm (Cell Calling); (6) Perform secondary analysis such as dimensionality reduction and clustering. The specific analysis process is shown in the following figure:
@figure
number = 4
title = "Information Analysis Flow Chart"
file = <url = resource/analysis_pipeline.png>
size = <width = 768; height = 300>
desc = " "

@subtitle
number = 1
Disembarkation data quality control
@paragraph
Cell Ranger is used for demultiplexing and quality control of offline data, mainly including the following steps:
(1) Use cellranger mkfastq to convert Illumina BCL files to FASTQ format;
(2) Correct Cell Barcodes from Read1 against the whitelist: 10X Chromium uses approximately 737,000 known Barcode sequences (whitelist), with minimum edit distance of 3 between whitelist Barcodes. Therefore, 1-base sequencing errors can be uniquely corrected back to the original whitelist sequence, and Reads with Barcodes that cannot be corrected are discarded;
(3) Correct UMI sequences: If two UMIs under the same gene and same Cell Barcode differ by only one base (edit distance=1), the UMI with fewer supporting Reads is corrected to the UMI with more supporting Reads;
(4) Sequencing quality assessment: Q30 Bases in Barcode should be ≥90%, Q30 Bases in UMI should be ≥90%, Q30 Bases in RNA Read should be ≥75%, and Valid Barcodes ratio should be greater than 75%.

@subtitle
number = 2
Data comparison and statistics
@paragraph
RNA reads (insertions) will be matched against a pre-constructed reference genome database using STAR comparison software. Based on the STAR comparison results, combined with the information in the reference dataset (gtf/gff file), reads coverage information of each region of the genome was analyzed to obtain the ratio information of exons, introns and intergene regions, which could be used as a reference index for data quality control. Cell Ranger integrates the STAR algorithm with optimizations for 10X Barcode structure. The following is a schematic diagram of its principle:
@figure
number = 5
title = "STAR Comparison Principle"
file = <url = resource/STAR.png>
size = <width = 500; height = 300>
desc = " "

@subtitle
number = 3
Quantitative statistics of gene expression
@paragraph
The quantitative analysis of gene expression of scRNA-seq is mainly based on UMI counting. Reads pairs with the same Cell Barcode sequence, UMI label, and alignment to the same gene would be defined as PCR duplicates. Only non-PCR duplicates and verified Cell Barcode and UMI are used for downstream gene quantitative analysis. UMI correction rules are as follows:
(1) Under the same Cell Barcode and same gene, if two UMIs differ by only one base (edit distance=1), the UMI with fewer supporting Reads is merged into the UMI with more supporting Reads;
(2) Each UMI group is ultimately counted as 1 mRNA molecule.

@subtitle
number = 4
Data quality control and filtering
@paragraph
Commonly used QC filtration conditions mainly include:
(1) The number of genes identified per cell: a) Low quality cells or empty droplets usually identify very few genes; b) Twin or polycellular droplets may exhibit an unusually high number of genes.
(2) The number of molecules identified per cell (similar to 1);
(3) The ratio to the mitochondrial genome;
Cell Ranger uses the EmptyDrops algorithm to automatically distinguish real cells from empty droplets. On this basis, commonly used cell filtration parameters for downstream analysis are:
(1) Filter out cells with a gene number less than 200 or greater than the maximum gene number *90%;
(2) Cells with more than 5% of mitochondrial reads were filtered out (cell types with high energy requirements required special treatment, such as tumor tissue and cardiomyocytes, and the general threshold was set to more than 20%);

@subtitle
number = 5
High variable feature screening
@paragraph
Calculating subsets that exhibit highly differentiated features between cells in the dataset (i.e., they are highly expressed in some cells and low expressed in others), focusing on these genes in downstream analysis helps highlight biological signals in single-cell datasets. By default, the 2,000 genes with the highest degree of variation are selected. These genes will be used for downstream analysis, such as PCA.

@subtitle
number = 6
Cell clustering
@paragraph
In factor analysis technology, variables are grouped according to their correlation, that is, all variables in a specific group have a high correlation with each other, but often have a low correlation with variables in other groups. Then, the most significant principal component is selected according to the degree of enrichment and P-value. According to tSNE or UMAP (https://www.nature.com/articles/nbt.4314) algorithm of nonlinear dimension reduction analysis, the cells together into classes. The UMAP algorithm is adopted by default for the following reasons: UMAP is more suitable for processing large data sets and complex dimensional information. Combines the power of visualization with the ability to reduce data dimensions; In addition to preserving the local structure, it also preserves the global structure of the data. (The Ultimate Guide to 12 Dimensionality Reduction Techniques (with Python codes))

@subtitle
number = 7
Screening of specific marker genes in cluster
@paragraph
The differentially expressed genes of each cluster and all remaining cells were calculated, and the screening parameters were as follows:
\verbatim
min.pct = 0.25, logfc.threshold = 0.25
\end

